1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:01,460
Forty six.

2
00:00:01,460 --> 00:00:02,644
Twenty three.

3
00:00:02,644 --> 00:00:03,654
Forty six.

4
00:00:04,054 --> 00:00:05,549
A cell from your skin.

5
00:00:05,549 --> 00:00:06,863
A sperm or an egg.

6
00:00:06,863 --> 00:00:08,829
The very first cell of a baby.

7
00:00:09,279 --> 00:00:11,058
Aarav loves number puzzles.

8
00:00:11,058 --> 00:00:15,378
He asked: if cells copy themselves,
why is the middle number half?

9
00:00:15,728 --> 00:00:18,365
The answer is a count of chromosome sets.

10
00:00:18,365 --> 00:00:22,284
And plants play this number game
in a stranger way than we do.

11
00:00:22,784 --> 00:00:27,272
By the end, you will read n and two
n, and know where a plant halves and

12
00:00:27,272 --> 00:00:28,774
doubles its chromosomes.

13
00:00:29,424 --> 00:00:31,229
First, what is being counted?

14
00:00:31,579 --> 00:00:36,245
Inside the nucleus sit chromosomes,
thin threads that carry the instructions

15
00:00:36,245 --> 00:00:37,287
of the cell.

16
00:00:37,737 --> 00:00:39,055
They come in sets.

17
00:00:39,055 --> 00:00:42,574
The letter n means the number
of chromosomes in one set.

18
00:00:43,024 --> 00:00:45,850
A cell with one set is haploid, written n.

19
00:00:45,850 --> 00:00:50,471
A cell with two sets, one from each
parent, is diploid, written two n.

20
00:00:50,471 --> 00:00:54,034
In humans, n is twenty
three, so two n is forty six.

21
00:00:54,684 --> 00:00:57,108
Let's zoom into an onion root, and count.

22
00:00:57,458 --> 00:01:00,088
The root tip is where cells divide fast.

23
00:01:00,588 --> 00:01:01,488
Zoom in.

24
00:01:01,324 --> 00:01:04,716
These are root tip cells,
each with its own nucleus.

25
00:01:05,216 --> 00:01:06,773
Zoom into one nucleus.

26
00:01:06,773 --> 00:01:08,624
The threads are chromosomes.

27
00:01:08,624 --> 00:01:10,593
Let's line them up and count.

28
00:01:11,093 --> 00:01:12,383
Onion has sixteen.

29
00:01:12,383 --> 00:01:16,125
They form eight pairs, one of
each pair from each parent.

30
00:01:16,125 --> 00:01:18,733
So n is eight, and two n is sixteen.

31
00:01:19,383 --> 00:01:22,179
Now let's run the two ways a cell divides.

32
00:01:22,179 --> 00:01:23,389
Watch the count.

33
00:01:23,739 --> 00:01:24,639
Step one.

34
00:01:24,625 --> 00:01:25,525
Mitosis.

35
00:01:25,391 --> 00:01:29,657
The chromosomes copy themselves,
and the cell splits in two.

36
00:01:30,407 --> 00:01:31,307
Step two.

37
00:01:31,219 --> 00:01:33,567
Each new cell gets the same count.

38
00:01:33,567 --> 00:01:35,003
Two n stays two n.

39
00:01:35,003 --> 00:01:38,025
A haploid cell can do this too: n stays n.

40
00:01:38,775 --> 00:01:39,825
Step three.

41
00:01:39,825 --> 00:01:40,725
Meiosis.

42
00:01:40,635 --> 00:01:45,610
A diploid cell divides twice, but
copies its chromosomes only once.

43
00:01:46,360 --> 00:01:47,274
Step four.

44
00:01:47,274 --> 00:01:48,725
The pairs separate.

45
00:01:48,725 --> 00:01:51,373
Four cells form, each with one set.

46
00:01:51,373 --> 00:01:52,728
Two n becomes n.

47
00:01:53,478 --> 00:01:54,418
Step five.

48
00:01:54,418 --> 00:01:57,063
Fertilisation joins two haploid cells.

49
00:01:57,063 --> 00:01:59,245
n plus n makes two n again.

50
00:01:59,995 --> 00:02:02,752
So here are the three
rules of the number game.

51
00:02:03,102 --> 00:02:05,842
Meiosis halves the count, from two n to n.

52
00:02:06,592 --> 00:02:09,439
Fertilisation doubles it, from n to two n.

53
00:02:10,189 --> 00:02:11,478
Mitosis copies it.

54
00:02:11,478 --> 00:02:15,337
The count stays the same,
whether the cell is n or two n.

55
00:02:16,087 --> 00:02:17,710
Now the plant twist.

56
00:02:17,710 --> 00:02:22,188
In plants, both haploid and
diploid cells can grow by mitosis.

57
00:02:22,538 --> 00:02:25,976
So a plant can build a whole
body from haploid cells.

58
00:02:25,976 --> 00:02:28,796
That is the haploid
phase, the gametophyte.

59
00:02:28,796 --> 00:02:30,438
It starts from a spore.

60
00:02:30,888 --> 00:02:33,200
And a whole body from diploid cells.

61
00:02:33,200 --> 00:02:35,887
That is the diploid phase, the sporophyte.

62
00:02:35,887 --> 00:02:37,526
It starts from a zygote.

63
00:02:37,976 --> 00:02:41,421
In us, the haploid phase is
just the sperm and the egg.

64
00:02:41,421 --> 00:02:44,767
In a moss, the haploid phase
is the whole green plant.

65
00:02:45,417 --> 00:02:46,317
Your turn.

66
00:02:46,296 --> 00:02:49,481
A cell from the green,
leafy part of a moss.

67
00:02:49,481 --> 00:02:51,766
One set of chromosomes, or two?

68
00:02:51,766 --> 00:02:53,060
Pause and decide.

69
00:02:56,260 --> 00:02:57,160
One set!

70
00:02:57,053 --> 00:03:02,366
The leafy moss grows from a spore by
mitosis, so every cell in it is haploid.

71
00:03:03,016 --> 00:03:04,342
Remember this.

72
00:03:04,342 --> 00:03:05,730
Meiosis halves.

73
00:03:05,730 --> 00:03:07,551
Fertilisation doubles.

74
00:03:07,551 --> 00:03:08,939
Mitosis copies.

75
00:03:09,539 --> 00:03:11,244
Now, a worked example.

76
00:03:11,244 --> 00:03:13,977
Onion leaf cells have sixteen chromosomes.

77
00:03:13,977 --> 00:03:15,743
Let's fill in its flower.

78
00:03:16,093 --> 00:03:19,254
Meiosis in the anther
leads to the pollen grain.

79
00:03:19,254 --> 00:03:20,314
Half: eight.

80
00:03:20,764 --> 00:03:23,573
Meiosis in the ovule leads to the egg.

81
00:03:23,573 --> 00:03:24,555
Also eight.

82
00:03:25,005 --> 00:03:27,897
A male gamete, eight,
joins the egg, eight.

83
00:03:27,897 --> 00:03:30,843
The zygote has sixteen,
and so does the embryo.

84
00:03:31,293 --> 00:03:34,995
The other male gamete joins
two polar nuclei, eight each.

85
00:03:34,995 --> 00:03:37,197
Three times eight is twenty four.

86
00:03:37,197 --> 00:03:38,950
The endosperm is three n.

87
00:03:39,600 --> 00:03:41,064
Now, the exam trap.

88
00:03:41,414 --> 00:03:45,723
Many students think a haploid
cell cannot divide by mitosis.

89
00:03:46,123 --> 00:03:47,810
But in plants it can.

90
00:03:47,810 --> 00:03:52,733
Spores divide by mitosis to build the
whole gametophyte, and the gametophyte

91
00:03:52,733 --> 00:03:55,116
makes its gametes by mitosis too.

92
00:03:55,766 --> 00:03:57,198
Where is this asked?

93
00:03:57,198 --> 00:04:02,001
As chromosome counts: given two n of
a plant, find the count in the pollen,

94
00:04:02,001 --> 00:04:04,567
the egg, the zygote and the endosperm.

95
00:04:05,167 --> 00:04:06,360
Exam level now.

96
00:04:06,360 --> 00:04:10,516
A flowering plant has twenty four
chromosomes in its leaf cells.

97
00:04:10,516 --> 00:04:12,548
Find the count in each part.

98
00:04:12,548 --> 00:04:13,792
Pause and solve.

99
00:04:19,192 --> 00:04:20,412
The answer is A.

100
00:04:20,412 --> 00:04:23,269
Twelve, twelve, twenty
four and thirty six.

101
00:04:23,769 --> 00:04:27,791
Pollen and egg come after meiosis:
half of twenty four is twelve.

102
00:04:28,291 --> 00:04:30,887
The zygote is n plus n, twenty four.

103
00:04:30,887 --> 00:04:34,736
The endosperm is three n:
three times twelve, thirty six.

104
00:04:35,336 --> 00:04:36,798
Here is your recap.

105
00:04:36,798 --> 00:04:38,301
Screenshot this card.

106
00:04:41,501 --> 00:04:44,500
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

107
00:04:44,900 --> 00:04:45,800
One.

108
00:04:45,372 --> 00:04:47,522
What does the letter n stand for?

109
00:04:48,422 --> 00:04:49,322
Two.

110
00:04:48,950 --> 00:04:51,807
Which division halves
the chromosome count?

111
00:04:52,707 --> 00:04:53,607
Three.

112
00:04:53,321 --> 00:04:56,960
Why can a plant build a whole
body from haploid cells?

113
00:04:58,060 --> 00:05:03,055
Next time: in a moss, a fern and a
mango tree, which phase do we actually

114
00:05:03,055 --> 00:05:03,955
see?

115
00:05:03,569 --> 00:05:06,893
We find out in life cycles
of major plant groups.
